\title{What Decides the Reported Accuracy? \\ An Empirical Study of LLM-Based Triton GPU Kernel Generation}

%% Ignored in review mode; acl.sty prints "Anonymous ACL submission" instead.
\author{First Author \\
  Affiliation / Address line 1 \\
  \texttt{email@domain} \\}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Many recent papers use large language models to write GPU kernels, and they report
accuracy on the same benchmark. The reported numbers, however, are very far apart.
Some papers report almost zero, and other papers report one hundred percent.
This paper does not propose a new generation system. Instead, we ask what causes this gap.
We measure 8 commercial models and 4 open models on three GPUs and on four benchmarks,
and we use one fixed evaluation harness for every run. We also take released artifacts from
earlier work, such as a Triton-specific reinforcement learning model, an agent framework,
a failure-rule memory, and profiling feedback, and we run them in our own setting.
We report eight findings. A large part of the gap does not come from the model at all.
It comes from the evaluation setup and from hidden limits in the serving stack. One such
defect alone moves accuracy from 0.45 to 0.75. Most of the rest of the gap comes from the
test-time compute budget. The same free 8B open model reaches 0.47 under a small budget and
0.95 under a large budget. A larger budget, however, only helps inside the training
distribution. Outside it, and on a new domain-specific language, a larger budget changes
nothing. We also find that breadth beats depth, and that domain specialization beats model
size. We close with the six axes that a paper should report together with its accuracy number.
\end{abstract}

%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

GPU kernels decide much of the cost of deep learning systems. Writing a fast kernel by hand
is hard, because the programmer must reason about the memory hierarchy, about thread
synchronization, and about warp-level behavior~\citep{kirk2016programming}. OpenAI
Triton~\citep{tillet2019triton} lowers this barrier. It offers a tile-based programming model in
Python and leaves many low-level choices to the compiler. Triton became the default backend of
\texttt{torch.compile} in PyTorch~2.0~\citep{ansel2024pytorch2}, so it is now a natural target for
automatic code generation. Many groups therefore try to generate Triton and CUDA kernels with
large language models, and they measure the result on
KernelBench~\citep{ouyang2024kernelbench}, which has become the standard benchmark.

The problem is that the reported numbers do not agree. KernelEvolve reports one hundred
percent~\citep{liao2026kernelevolve}. CudaForge reports 97.6 percent~\citep{cudaforge2025}. QiMeng
reports near-perfect accuracy on the first two levels~\citep{qimengkernel2026}. At the same time,
other studies on the same benchmark report single-digit accuracy. A reader cannot tell whether
this gap comes from model ability, from search budget, or from the measurement setup itself.
Each paper uses a different number of attempts, a different verification strength, and a
different serving stack, and most papers do not report these settings at all.

This paper is therefore not a system paper. We do not propose a new generator, a new agent,
or a new training method. We audit the current state of the field. We fix one evaluation
harness and then change one axis at a time on top of it. We vary the model, the GPU, the
benchmark, and the compute budget. We also take artifacts that earlier work has released,
including a Triton-specific reinforcement learning model~\citep{li2025autotriton}, a multi-turn
reinforcement learning model together with its training environment~\citep{drkernel2026}, an open
deep-agent framework, a failure-rule memory~\citep{du2026adaexplore}, and profiling
feedback~\citep{cudaforge2025}. We run each of them in our own setting and we report where the
reported benefit holds and where it breaks. We organize the study around five questions.
RQ1 asks how accurate current models really are under one common setting. RQ2 asks how much
the measurement setup alone decides the reported number. RQ3 asks what the failures look like
and how they change as models improve. RQ4 asks what actually raises accuracy, that is, depth
of repair, breadth of sampling, test-time budget, or reinforcement learning. RQ5 asks whether
the artifacts of earlier work transfer to another harness and to another distribution.

We organized more than one hundred related papers into eight groups, namely CUDA generation,
Triton generation, reinforcement learning, agent and evolutionary frameworks, compiler and IR
level work, benchmarks, evaluation methodology, and supervised foundation models. Two gaps
became visible. First, many papers propose a single technique, but we did not find a paper that
compares several models, several GPUs, and several benchmarks under one controlled harness.
Second, papers that report perfect accuracy always assume a large attempt budget, such as
unbounded tree search, repeated repair with profiling, or large-scale reinforcement learning,
yet they do not report the single-attempt number next to it. The individual parts that we use,
such as iterative repair, best-of-$N$ sampling, model cascading, and failure-rule reuse, are all
known. What is new here is the controlled measurement on top of them.

Our contribution is a set of findings rather than a new method, and we state them here so that
this section alone is enough to see the result of the study. First, the newest commercial
flagship models converge to almost the same behavior below a certain difficulty, and a free 8B
Triton-specific open model reaches the same level, so specialization beats size. Second,
changing only the evaluation infrastructure and the hidden serving limits, and not the model,
moves accuracy by up to 0.30, so accuracy numbers are not comparable across papers unless
these settings are stated. Third, as models get better, the dominant failure moves from
compilation errors to value mismatches, which also explains why profiling feedback does not
help before correctness is solved. Fourth, independent samples help more than deeper repair of
a single sample. Fifth, the gap between our full-benchmark numbers and the perfect scores in
the literature is mostly a difference of budget regimes, and reinforcement learning and budget
are complements rather than substitutes. Sixth, an agent framework improves search efficiency
but does not raise the ceiling of the base model, so a strong open model with an open framework
can reach a perfect score without any commercial model. Seventh, aggressive quantization
destroys quality on harder tasks, while full precision on a very large model destroys
throughput, so a mid-size model at full precision is the practical choice. Eighth, and most
important as a limit of the whole picture, a larger budget only helps inside the training
distribution. Out-of-distribution tasks and a new domain-specific language do not improve at
all. From these findings we derive six axes that future papers should report next to accuracy.

%% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Benchmarks and learning.}
KernelBench~\citep{ouyang2024kernelbench} defines 250 tasks in three levels and asks the model to
turn a PyTorch reference into an equivalent GPU kernel. TritonBench~\citep{li2025tritonbench}
collects real Triton operators from GitHub, and TritonGym~\citep{tritongym2026} standardizes tool
access so that model ability and workflow design can be measured apart from each other.
Several surveys track this fast-moving
area~\citep{yu2026automated_kernel_survey, zhang2026compilerstack, gong2025codeopt_survey}.
On the learning side, AutoTriton~\citep{li2025autotriton} combines supervised training and GRPO and
reports commercial-level quality from an 8B model. TritonRL~\citep{woo2025tritonrl} uses verifiable
rewards to suppress reward hacking, and DRTriton~\citep{drtriton2025} uses large synthetic data.
For CUDA, CUDA-L1~\citep{cudal1_2026} uses contrastive reinforcement learning and
Kevin~\citep{baronio2025kevin} uses multi-turn reinforcement learning. \citet{tehrani2026gpt5kernel} fine-tune a frontier model and raise single-attempt accuracy.
Dr.\,Kernel~\citep{drkernel2026} names two failure modes of kernel reinforcement learning, namely
reward hacking and lazy optimization, and releases both a distributed environment and a
multi-turn advantage estimator. CUDA Agent~\citep{dai2026cudaagent} and
MusaCoder~\citep{cheng2026musacoder} extend this line to large agentic training and to a new
accelerator. All of these methods need data curation and a large amount of compute. We do not
train anything. We download what these papers release, we run it in our harness, and we ask
whether the reported benefit survives a change of harness and a change of distribution.

\paragraph{Agent frameworks.}
A second line uses a fixed model inside a repair loop and adds no training.
GEAK~\citep{wang2025geak} uses a generator, a reflector, an evaluator, and an optimizer.
CudaForge~\citep{cudaforge2025} adds hardware profiling metrics. EGG~\citep{egg2026} splits kernel
generation into algorithm design and hardware tuning, and KernelBand~\citep{kernelband2026} treats
optimization as a bandit problem. STARK~\citep{stark2026} separates planning, coding, and
debugging, and PRAGMA~\citep{lei2025pragma} turns profiling data into explicit reasoning steps.
AccelOpt~\citep{accelopt2025} reuses an experience memory, and
KernelEvolve~\citep{liao2026kernelevolve} runs tree search across several hardware platforms.
AdaExplore~\citep{du2026adaexplore} distills repeated failures into reusable rules and keeps search
diverse. KForge~\citep{sereda2026kforge} and Kernel Foundry~\citep{huang2026kernelfoundry} alternate
a correctness pass and an optimization pass. Other work reduces the reasoning load by exposing a
compact language instead of raw CUDA, such as $\mu$CUTLASS~\citep{hari2026ucutlass},
CuTeGen~\citep{saba2026cutegen}, and QiMeng-Kernel~\citep{qimengkernel2026}, or by adding hardware
context, such as SwizzlePerf~\citep{swizzleperf2025}. LEGO~\citep{lego2026} moves from single kernels
to whole model graphs. These frameworks all assume a large attempt budget, and this is exactly
the axis that we control.

\paragraph{Verification and evaluation.}
A generated kernel can compile and still be wrong, so verification is now a topic of its own.
\citet{kwon2026cove} build a verification chain between the compiler and the runtime.
COLT~\citep{tang2026colt} lets several small models cooperate and escalates to a larger model when
needed, and \citet{crupi2026slmjudge} train a small model as a judge to pick a correct
candidate cheaply. AutoMegaKernel~\citep{jaber2026automegakernel} rejects wrong proposals before
execution with a static checker, and AgentCompile~\citep{li2026agentcompile} keeps the correctness
decision inside the compiler. The risk of an unverified number is real, because inflated results
from reward hacking have already been reported~\citep{lange2025aicudaengineer}. We extend this
concern from verification to the measurement setup as a whole.

%% ============================================================
\section{Study Design}\label{sec:design}

\input{fig_overview}

Figure~\ref{fig:overview} shows the structure of the study. Every run shares one harness. The
harness builds the prompt, calls the model, extracts the code from the answer, scores the kernel
inside an isolated subprocess, classifies the error, and then repairs and retries. The isolation
step is not an optimization. The CUDA runtime keeps state per process, so a single bad kernel can
corrupt the context and make every later evaluation in the same process fail. Without isolation a
sequential run over 250 tasks is not meaningful. For correctness we draw five random inputs and
require an absolute error below $10^{-2}$ against the reference. For speed we take the median of
ten timed runs and report Fast-0, Fast-1, and Fast-2 as defined by the
benchmark~\citep{ouyang2024kernelbench}.

We change four axes on top of this fixed harness. The first axis is the model. We use 8
commercial models, namely gpt-4o-mini, gpt-4o, gpt-5.2, gpt-5.2-pro, gpt-5.4-nano, gpt-5.5,
gemini-3.1-pro-preview, and claude-opus-4-7. We also use 4 open models, namely
Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B~\citep{hui2024qwencoder}, AutoTriton-8B~\citep{li2025autotriton}, a quantized
GLM-5.2 in a 2-bit and a 4-bit version, and Dr.\,Kernel-14B~\citep{drkernel2026}. The second axis is
the hardware. We use an RTX PRO 6000 with 96GB, an H100 with 80GB, and an A6000 with 48GB. The
third axis is the benchmark. We use KernelBench with 250 tasks, TritonBench with 166 tasks,
TritonGym with 139 standard tasks and 13 out-of-distribution tasks, and FlashInfer-Bench with 8
dense tasks~\citep{flashinferbench2025}. The fourth axis is the compute budget. We vary the number
of samples, the number of repair rounds, and the number of effective turns in a multi-turn setting
from 1 up to 14. Commercial models run through a unified gateway. Open models run on a local vLLM
server, or on llama.cpp for the quantized model.

The software stack is Triton 3.5.1 on the H100 and Triton 3.6 on the RTX PRO 6000, with PyTorch 2.x,
Python 3.12, and CUDA 12.x. We call a budget \emph{small} when we take one sample and up to three
repair rounds, and we call it \emph{large} when we take three samples and up to nine repair rounds,
which is at most 27 attempts per task.

%% ============================================================
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{RQ1: How Accurate Are Current Models?}\label{sec:rq1}

We first fix the small budget and run every model on all 100 tasks of KernelBench Level~1.
Table~\ref{tab:rq1} reports the result. We exclude any run that only covered a subset of the
tasks, because such a run cannot be compared on equal terms.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\caption{Accuracy on all 100 tasks of KernelBench Level~1 under the small budget.
Every row uses the same harness and the same task set.}
\label{tab:rq1}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
\headrow{Model} & \headrow{Type} & \headrow{Fast-0} \\
\midrule
gpt-4o-mini            & commercial          & 15\% \\
gpt-4o                 & commercial          & 19\% \\
Qwen3-Coder-30B        & open, 30B           & 27\% \\
gpt-5.4-nano           & commercial          & 35\% \\
gpt-5.5                & commercial          & 37\% \\
gemini-3.1-pro-preview & commercial          & 43\% \\
claude-opus-4-7        & commercial flagship & 46\% \\
\midrule
AutoTriton-8B          & open, 8B, RL        & \textbf{47\%} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The effect of model generation is clear. Accuracy grows from 19 percent for gpt-4o to between 43
and 46 percent for the newest flagship models. The last row is the more interesting one. A free
open model with only 8B parameters, trained specifically for Triton, reaches the same level as the
best commercial flagship. We also observe that gpt-5.5 and claude-opus-4-7 solve exactly the same
37 tasks among the first 44 tasks. Their success and failure pattern is almost identical, and the
two models only separate on the last part of the benchmark. The model gemini-3.1-pro-preview shows
the same late recovery, so this behavior looks like a property of the current flagship generation
rather than a property of one model.

\finding{1}{Recent commercial flagship models converge to nearly the same behavior below a certain
difficulty, and a free 8B Triton-specific open model reaches that same level. For kernel
generation, domain specialization beats model size.}

These absolute numbers, between 15 and 47 percent, are far below the perfect scores in the
literature. The next two subsections show that most of this gap comes from axes other than model
ability.

\subsection{RQ2: How Much Does the Setup Decide?}\label{sec:rq2}

While auditing our own pipeline we found a series of defects that lower accuracy without any
relation to model ability. Table~\ref{tab:rq2} lists them together with the recovery after the fix.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{7pt}
\caption{Defects in the measurement setup and the recovery after the fix. In every row the model
stays the same and only the environment changes.}
\label{tab:rq2}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.20\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.28\textwidth}l@{}}
\toprule
\headrow{Defect} & \headrow{Symptom} & \headrow{Fix} & \headrow{Accuracy} \\
\midrule
GPU memory contention & serving holds 90\% of the GPU, so large inputs fail with out of memory
  & lower the serving share and free tensors per trial & 0.45 to 0.75 \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
No input dtype contract & the model assumes fp16 while the reference is fp32
  & state the dtype contract in the prompt & \multirow{2}{*}{0.65 to 1.00} \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
No gating, no context reset & a polluted context blocks every repair round
  & gate on correctness and restart from a clean context & \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
Framework token cap & the reasoning trace is cut before the code block
  & raise the response token limit & 0.37 to 0.78 \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
Artifact generation cap & the served limit clamps the client limit
  & fix the generation config of the artifact & truncation gone \\
\addlinespace[2pt]
Serving context length & the repair prompt overflows and every round is lost
  & enlarge the serving context & failure gone \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The first row is the clearest case. When the serving process holds about 90 percent of the GPU,
the scorer runs out of memory on tasks with large inputs. One Level~1 task on ReLU has a single
input tensor of 6.4GB. As a result the harness recorded a failure even for ReLU, which is
semantically trivial. Lowering the serving share and freeing tensors after every trial raised
accuracy from 0.45 to 0.75 with no change to the model. The other rows have the same character.
The harness counts them as tasks that the model failed to solve, but in fact the code was cut
before it arrived, or the scorer broke down on its own.

Three of these are hidden variables. The response token cap of the framework, the generation cap
inside the released model artifact, and the serving context length never appear in any accuracy
report. A reasoning model spends thousands of tokens on its thinking trace before it writes any
code, so a common 8K cap turns a solvable task into an empty answer.

\finding{2}{Changing only the evaluation infrastructure and the hidden serving limits, without
touching the model, moves accuracy by as much as 0.30. Accuracy numbers from different systems are
therefore not comparable unless these settings are reported.}

We also ported the failure-rule memory of earlier work~\citep{du2026adaexplore}, which distills each
failure into one short rule and reuses it across tasks. The rules that the system collected while
the memory defect was still active were wrong, for example a rule that said the GPU does not have
enough memory to run the kernel. These wrong rules had high weight and filled the prompt, and the
model then failed even on a plain square matrix multiplication. Accuracy recovered after we cleared
the store. Experience reuse is therefore only an asset when the evaluation environment is already
healthy, and a shared store spreads the damage across servers.

\subsection{RQ3: What Do the Failures Look Like?}\label{sec:rq3}

We classify each failure as a compilation error, a value mismatch, a CUDA error, or a timeout.
Table~\ref{tab:rq3} reports the distribution.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\caption{Share of each error type among failed tasks on KernelBench Level~1. The timeout column
counts our own response and evaluation time limits together with unclassified runtime errors such
as API rate limits. It is not a constraint of the benchmark.}
\label{tab:rq3}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\headrow{Model} & \headrow{Compile} & \headrow{Value} & \headrow{CUDA} & \headrow{Timeout} \\
\midrule
gpt-4o-mini     & 71.8\% & 20.0\% & 2.4\%  & 2.4\%  \\
gpt-4o          & 45.7\% & 37.0\% & 11.1\% & 0\%    \\
gpt-5.2         & 6.9\%  & 58.6\% & 1.7\%  & 20.7\% \\
claude-opus-4-7 & 0\%    & 65\%   & 0\%    & 28\%   \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The dominant error moves in a regular way as the model gets better. Compilation errors fall from
71.8 percent to 0 percent, while value mismatches rise from 20 percent to 65 percent. Learning the
syntax of Triton is comparatively easy for a language model, but understanding the numerical
meaning of a GPU operation stays hard.

This diagnosis has a practical consequence. We ran an A/B test on three models in which we injected
Nsight Compute hardware counters into the optimization prompt. Accuracy did not improve for any
model, and the speedup gain was very small. A profiling signal only applies to a kernel that is
already correct, while the real bottleneck is correctness. Profiling-driven optimization therefore
only makes sense after correctness is solved.

\finding{3}{As models improve, failures move from syntactic errors to semantic errors. Profiling
feedback has no effect before correctness is solved, so a correctness-first and profiling-later
design is required.}

The timeout column needs a separate reading. For the reasoning model gpt-5.2-pro, 100 percent of
the Level~1 failures were timeouts. This was not a limit of ability. The long thinking trace simply
did not fit inside our response time limit, so this is an operational constraint of the setup.

\subsection{RQ4: What Actually Raises Accuracy?}\label{sec:rq4}

\subsubsection{Depth versus breadth.}
We ran gpt-4o on Level~1 with 1, 3, 5, and 10 repair rounds. The individual runs solved 19, 21, 19,
and 22 tasks, so deeper repair alone barely helped. The union of the four runs, however, solved 28
tasks, which is 27.3 percent above the best single run. Each run therefore solves some tasks that
the other runs miss. Table~\ref{tab:rq4a} also shows that the error profile shifts with depth. More
rounds remove compilation errors but leave value mismatches behind, which matches Finding~3.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\caption{Effect of the number of repair rounds for gpt-4o on KernelBench Level~1.}
\label{tab:rq4a}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}
\toprule
\headrow{Rounds} & \headrow{Solved} & \headrow{Compile err.} & \headrow{Value mism.} \\
\midrule
1  & 19 & 59.3\% & 24.7\% \\
3  & 21 & 51.9\% & 39.2\% \\
5  & 19 & 45.7\% & 37.0\% \\
10 & 22 & 32.1\% & 53.8\% \\
\midrule
Union & \textbf{28} & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The same effect holds across models. In a cascade, gpt-4o alone solved 25 tasks, and adding the next
generation model raised the total to 61 tasks, which is a factor of 2.44. The union of five models
reached 51 out of 100 and beat the best single model at 46 out of 100 by 10.9 percent. The gain was
not uniform. Adding an expensive reasoning model as the last stage added only one task out of 225,
so its cost was not justified. We also note that one recent flagship model alone, at 46 out of 100,
already beats the earlier three-model cascade at 43 out of 100, so a better model generation reduces
the need for a multi-model strategy.

\finding{4}{Breadth beats depth. Given the same budget, several independent attempts are more
useful than deeper repair of one attempt.}

\subsubsection{Test-time budget.}
When we change only the budget axis, most of the gap disappears. Table~\ref{tab:rq4b} shows that the
free 8B model moves from 0.47 under the small budget to 0.95 under the large budget, which is close
to the perfect scores in the literature.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\caption{Level~1 accuracy when only the budget changes. Small budget is one sample and three rounds.
Large budget is three samples and nine rounds. The value marked with a dagger is computed over the
38 tasks that finished inside the time limit.}
\label{tab:rq4b}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrr@{}}
\toprule
\headrow{Budget} & \headrow{Scope} & \headrow{Qwen-30B} & \headrow{AT-8B} & \headrow{opus} \\
\midrule
small & all 100     & 0.27 & 0.47 & --- \\
large & first 20    & 0.75 & \textbf{0.95} & \textbf{1.00} \\
large & all 100     & ---  & 0.76$^{\dagger}$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A larger budget, however, is limited by wall-clock time. In the full run with the large budget, 27
attempts per task did not fit into a two-hour limit on a slow local server, and 59 percent of the
tasks did not finish. The real bottleneck of test-time scaling is therefore not the number of
attempts but the throughput per attempt. This also explains why papers that report perfect accuracy
assume a fast commercial API or a large serving cluster.

\subsubsection{Reinforcement learning and budget.}
We reproduced a released multi-turn reinforcement learning environment and changed only the number
of effective turns from 1 to 14. Table~\ref{tab:rq4c} compares a model without such training and a
model with it.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\caption{Fast@1.2 in percent as a function of the number of effective turns. Only the turn count
changes. DrK-14B is trained with kernel-specific reinforcement learning and Qwen3-30B is not.}
\label{tab:rq4c}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\headrow{Model and level} & \headrow{1 turn} & \headrow{3 turns} & \headrow{7 turns} & \headrow{14 turns} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-30B, L2 & 3.7  & 11.9 & 16.8 & 28.4 \\
DrK-14B, L2   & 17.4 & 41.3 & 60.6 & \textbf{76.9} \\
DrK-14B, L1   & 11.5 & 22.0 & 27.0 & 31.2 \\
DrK-14B, L3   & 3.0  & 8.3  & 13.0 & 20.5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Both models improve monotonically with the budget, so budget dominance is a general effect and not a
property of one model. The trained model, however, raises both the intercept, by a factor of 4.7 at
one turn, and the gain per turn, by 59.5 points against 24.7 points. The saturation point also depends
on difficulty. Level~1 saturates around seven turns, while Level~3 has not saturated even at fourteen
turns and shows the largest relative gain.

\finding{5}{The perfect scores in the literature and our full-benchmark numbers are measurements of
different budget regimes, not of different model ability. Reinforcement learning and test-time budget
are complements, and the saturation point depends on task difficulty.}

\subsection{RQ5: What Transfers to Another Setting?}\label{sec:rq5}

\subsubsection{Reusing a failure-rule memory.}
Beyond the poisoning case in Section~\ref{sec:rq2}, the failure-rule memory is useful when the
harness is healthy. The system turns each failure into one short rule, for example a rule that says
the kernel must not call PyTorch operations inside the Triton kernel body, and it keeps these rules
across tasks. During one full evaluation the store collected between 73 and 80 distinct rules, and
the rules covered common mistakes such as wrong tile dimensions, a wrong block size along the
reduction axis, and missing contiguity assumptions. This kind of memory is cheap, since it only
needs one extra model call per failure, and it is the most portable of all the artifacts that we
reused. Its risk, however, is asymmetric. A useful rule saves a few attempts, while a wrong rule
that carries a high weight can block a whole class of tasks, as we saw when accuracy collapsed on
tasks as simple as a square matrix multiplication.

\subsubsection{Reproducing a released training environment.}
We first downloaded the official code and the released weights of a kernel-specific reinforcement
learning study and checked whether the reported numbers reproduce. On the 100 tasks of Level~2 we
measured Fast@1.2 at 24.3 percent against 25.6 percent in the paper, and our Fast@1.5 and Fast@2
were even higher. The reported result is therefore real. Reproduction, however, needed an
eight-GPU node. On a single GPU the trainer and the serving engine did not fit together, so
reproducibility does not imply accessibility.

\subsubsection{Cross-hardware and cross-benchmark results.}
Table~\ref{tab:rq5} reports two open models on two GPUs across all levels. The advantage of the
8B Triton-specific model is consistent, and the difficulty cliff at Level~2 and Level~3 is sharp.
Accuracy falls from 0.47 on single operations to 0.10 on fused operations and to 0.06 on whole
models. On the real serving kernels of FlashInfer-Bench only one combination reaches 0.25 while
the others stay at zero, which points to differences in Triton version and compute architecture.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\caption{Open models on two GPUs across all levels under the small budget. PRO~6000 is the RTX PRO
6000 and AT-8B is the Triton-specific model. On the H100 the 30B model leaves too little memory for
the scorer, so the value marked with a dagger is depressed by out-of-memory failures. See Finding~2.}
\label{tab:rq5}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\multirow{2}{*}{\headrow{Level}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\headrow{Qwen-30B}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\headrow{AT-8B}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(l){4-5}
 & PRO 6000 & H100 & PRO 6000 & H100 \\
\midrule
L1, 100 tasks    & 0.27 & 0.12$^{\dagger}$ & \textbf{0.47} & 0.43 \\
L2, 100 tasks    & 0.05 & 0.06 & \textbf{0.10} & 0.08 \\
L3, 50 tasks     & 0.04 & 0.06 & \textbf{0.06} & 0.02 \\
FlashInfer, 8    & 0.00 & 0.00 & 0.00 & \textbf{0.25} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Accuracy itself is almost independent of the GPU as long as memory is sufficient. Under the same
large budget on the first 20 tasks, the 30B open model reached 0.75 on the RTX PRO 6000 and 0.60 on
the H100, and the whole difference came from memory. The H100 has 80GB, so after serving the 30B
model the scorer only had about 43GB, while the RTX PRO 6000 with 96GB left about 52GB. After we
correct for the out-of-memory failures the H100 number rises to about 0.70. A third GPU, the A6000
with 48GB, gave a similar profile across levels, with 0.37 on Level~1, 0.12 on Level~2, and 0.04 on
Level~3, which supports the same conclusion. The A6000, however, produced only about one fourth of
the throughput of the H100, so the same sweep took about three times longer in wall-clock time.

Speedup behaves in the opposite way. On a square matrix multiplication we measured 1.61 times on the
RTX PRO 6000 and 0.83 times on the H100, because the cuBLAS baseline of the H100 is very strong. A
speedup number is therefore only comparable inside one hardware setting. The quality of the
optimization also improves with model generation. Among correct kernels, the share that reaches at
least a two times speedup grows from 6.3 percent for gpt-4o-mini and 16.0 percent for gpt-4o to 28.6
percent for gpt-5.4-nano and 30.4 percent for claude-opus-4-7. The largest single speedup that we
observed was 84.03 times on one Level~3 task, which shows that a generated Triton kernel can
sometimes beat the default PyTorch implementation by a wide margin.

\subsubsection{Agent structure against model ability.}
We downloaded an open deep-agent framework that reports perfect accuracy and ran it with our own
open models. With Qwen-30B it solved 15 of the first 20 tasks, which is exactly the same score that
our own pipeline reaches under the large budget, and even the set of failed tasks was identical.
The wall-clock time, however, dropped from several hours to about one hour. With a stronger open
model, namely GLM-5.2 in 4-bit form, the same framework solved all 20 tasks and matched the
commercial flagship.

\finding{6}{An agent framework improves search efficiency but does not raise the ceiling of the
base model. Accuracy is a product of framework efficiency and model ability, and a strong open
model with an open framework can reach a perfect score without any commercial model.}

Extending this combination to all of Level~1 gave 0.48 over the 77 tasks that we ran. Successes
concentrated on matrix multiplication, reduction, and element-wise tasks, while convolution and
sequential scan tasks dropped sharply. To separate budget from ability we removed truncation
completely, with a 32K serving context and a response limit of 24000 tokens, and retried three
representative convolution tasks. Truncation disappeared, yet all three still failed to converge.
The remaining convolution failures are therefore a limit of model ability on indexing large tensors,
not a limit of the token budget.

\subsubsection{Model size against weight precision.}
We loaded a very large open reasoning model, GLM-5.2, on an eight-GPU node with layer splitting. In
2-bit form it reached only 0.10 on Level~1 and solved nothing at all on Level~2, Level~3, and the
serving kernels, after 21.5 hours of compute. A general reasoning model with two orders of magnitude
more parameters therefore stayed below half of the 8B specialized model in Table~\ref{tab:rq5}. To
confirm that quantization was the cause, we reloaded the same model in 4-bit form. Accuracy recovered
to 0.96 over the tasks that finished inside the time limit, which is the best result among our open
models. This version, however, generated only about 8 tokens per second, so 76 percent of the tasks
exceeded the limit. The bottleneck simply moved from precision to throughput.

\finding{7}{Quantization loss grows with task complexity. Low precision destroys quality and full
precision on a very large model destroys throughput, so a mid-size model at full precision is the
practical choice under a fixed budget.}

\subsubsection{The boundary of budget.}
All results above hold inside the training distribution. To find the boundary we moved a
kernel-specific reinforcement learning model into a standardized agent environment and compared a
single attempt against a test-time scaling agent. On the 139 standard tasks Pass@1 rose from 12.2
percent to 18.0 percent, and the performance of the passing kernels relative to the oracle rose
from 0.143 to 0.285. On the 13 out-of-distribution tasks, however, Pass@1 stayed at 7.7 percent and
did not move at all. On a new domain-specific language the same model solved none of the three tasks
that our environment could run. The same pattern appears across benchmarks. On the Triton-specific
benchmark the advantage of the specialized model disappears and all four combinations converge into
a narrow band between 0.15 and 0.19.

To separate the cause we ran a two-by-two ablation over the prompt format and the budget. Changing
only the prompt format had almost no effect, and execution accuracy even fell from 0.151 to 0.127.
Turning on the budget, in contrast, raised execution accuracy by about 2.3 times and call accuracy
by about 2.5 times. The gap against the original paper is therefore explained by single-attempt
evaluation against repeated repair, and not by a mismatch with the training prompt. This repeats
what we found on KernelBench, so budget dominance is a general effect and not a property of one
benchmark.

\finding{8}{A larger test-time budget only improves accuracy and performance inside the
distribution. It does not rescue the collapse outside the distribution. The benefit of
reinforcement learning is also tied to its own training harness and transfers only in part.}

%% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper does not propose a new kernel generation system. It audits the current state of
LLM-based Triton kernel generation with five research questions. We measured 8 commercial models
and 4 open models on three GPUs and on four benchmarks with one fixed harness, and we ran released
artifacts of earlier work in our own setting. The reported accuracy of a system is not a function
of model ability alone. It is a function of ability, of test-time budget, and of the measurement
setup. Removing infrastructure defects, giving a sufficient budget, and using a specialized open
model is enough to reach the level of the best reported results without any commercial model.
This recipe, however, only works inside the training distribution, and no budget helps outside it.
We therefore ask that future work report six axes next to any accuracy number: the attempt budget,
the verification strength, the memory headroom of the scorer, the serving context length, the
response token caps of both the framework and the model artifact, and the evaluation distribution.
Our own full-benchmark numbers should be read as a budget-controlled floor rather than as a ceiling
of ability.

%% ============================================================
%% ARR requires a section titled exactly "Limitations" after the conclusion.
%% It does not count toward the page limit.
\section*{Limitations}

Some of our large-budget measurements ran on the first 20 tasks of Level~1 rather than on all 100
tasks, and the full run left unfinished tasks because of the wall-clock limit. Commercial models sit
behind an API, so we cannot control their serving conditions, and an uncontrolled infrastructure
difference remains in every comparison between commercial and open models. The out-of-distribution
set has only 13 tasks, so it shows that a boundary exists but does not measure its size precisely.
Finally, our timeout column mixes true time limits with unclassified runtime errors, so it should be
read as an operational category rather than as a single failure cause.

\section*{Ethical Considerations}

This study does not involve human subjects, personal data, or annotators. All benchmarks and
model artifacts that we reuse are publicly released by their authors for research use, and we cite
each of them. The main risk of the work is misuse of generated kernels in production without
verification, which we address by making verification strength one of the six reporting axes.
We report the
wall-clock cost of each experiment in Appendix~\ref{sec:app-budget} so that the compute can be
weighed against the findings.

